  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION...\n\nSecondary cold front will cross the region this evening. Another\nround or band of showers will cross the forecast area, then\ntransitioning to upslope showers. Had to blend in a little CAM\nguidance to get shower coverage this evening. Overnight some\nlight snow accumulation will be possible for the highest\nelevations. So there could be some snowliage in the morning. It\nwill also be a race to see how quickly we can decouple the cold\nadvection winds. NBM guidance is quite cold overnight, with MOS\nguidance warmer. I think the NBM is probably leaning a bit too\nhard on bias correction from past nights, so I brought lows up a\nbit. This keeps frost mostly out of the active zones. So no\nheadlines currently, but if we do decouple early it may be\nsomething to consider for the evening shift.\n\nSat will bring shallow mixing and fair weather as the strong\nhigh settles overhead. This will set up Sat night to likely be\nthe colder of the two nights. Despite high center drifting east\nand return flow developing aloft, the boundary layer should\ndecouple quickly around sunset. I blended in MOS guidance which\nwas cooler than NBM, so that areas of frost make it to the coast\nfrom Portland north. Headlines will likely be needed for the\nfinal night of the frost/freeze program.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION...\n\nToday's model guidance remains in good agreement in continuing \nto lift higher moisture associated with the remnants of Isaias \ntoward the Mid-Atlantic and Northeast and a weak low developing \nnear or just south of southern New England. This will bringing \nincreasing rain chances Sunday evening into at least the first \npart of Monday, but there's still uncertainty in the northward \nextent of measurable rain. Southernmost NH remains on track to \nsee the higher rain totals locally with box and whisker plots \nfrom ensembles favoring around 0.50\" of rain possible, although \nthere are still some juicier ensemble members suggesting upwards\nof an inch. Either way, hydro issues are not anticipated. \nRainfall amounts diminish with northward extent, and then a \ndrying trend should occur by Monday afternoon as the system \npulls away to the east. A cold front approaching from the north \ncould also bring some showers to the mountains late in the day \nor Monday night. \n\nTurning to Tuesday, a low pressure and cold front is forecast \nto approach New England from the Great Lakes, bringing more \nshowers Tuesday into at least Tuesday night. NBM PoP is already \n60-80% NH and western ME, which is supported by the ensembles, \nbut timing is the more uncertain aspect. The GFS is much faster \nin clearing the front, which would make Wednesday generally dry,\ncooler, and breezy while on the other hand the ECMWF camp is \nslower and keeps some rain into Wednesday. Regardless of the \nspeed of the system, cooler air will be drawn in behind this \nsystem, which could bring in some higher elevation snow showers \neither Wednesday or Wednesday night. \n\nHigh pressure is favored for Thursday to bring a dry day, and \nthen global models are already in good agreement for this far \nout showing a low moving across Quebec and sending a cold front \nour way to bring the next chance of showers on Friday.",
